e in RGB space and the masked image in HSV color space in a 1X3 plot. \n","\n","\n","b) Import the \"fruits.jpg\" image \n","\n","<img src = \"Images/fruits.jpg\" style=\"width:300px;height:180px\">\n","\n","You want to extract the orange colors from the image above. Apply a suitable mask to extract the orange pixels in RGB color space. \n","\n","You will now do the same thing in HSV space. Covert the same image to HSV space and apply a suitable mask to extract the orange color pixels.  \n","\n","Display the original image, the masked image in RGB space and the masked image in HSV color space in a 1X3 plot. "],"cell_type":"markdown"},{"id":"O0ok7xCl","metadata":{},"source":["# Task 3 (20 points)\n","\n","Import the \"pumpkins.jpeg\" image and apply the following point processing to it.\n","<img src = \"Images/pumpkins.jpeg\" style=\"width:250px;height:200px\">\n","\n","a) Create a darker image by subtracting 100 from each pixel intensity value.\n","\n","b) Create a nonlinear lower contrast image by applying the formula $(x / 255)^{1/3}$ X 255, where x is the original pixel intensity.\n","\n","c) Invert the original image where the pixel values are updated as 255 - current pixel intensity value.\n","\n","d) Create a brighter image by adding 100 to each pixel intensity value.\n","\n","e) Create a nonlinear higher contrast image by applying the formula $(x / 255)^{2}$ X 255, where x is the original pixel intensity.\n","\n","Display all of these images along with the original image in a 2X3 grid."],"cell_type":"markdown"},{"id":"WkqIjUmP","metadata":{},"source":["### Task 4 (30 points) \n","\n","Import the \"butterfly.jpg\" image \n","<img src = \"Images/butterfly.jpg\" style=\"width:300px;height:200px\">\n","\n","a) Perform average blur on the imported image with kernel size 5X5 and 11X11. Display the original image with the filtered/processed images in a 1X3 grid.\n","\n","\n","b) Add salt and pepper noise as 10% of all pixels. Perform gaussian blur with kernel size 5X5, sigma (standard deviation of the Gaussian) of 2.0 and another gaussian blur with kernel size 11X11, sigma 5. Display the image with salt and pepper noise and the filtered/processed images in a 1X3 grid.\n","\n","\n","c) Add salt and pepper noise as 20% of all pixels. Perform median blur with kernel size 5X5 and 11X11. Display the image with salt and pepper noise and the filtered/processed images in a 1X3 grid.\n","\n","\n","d) Resize the original image to 50X50 pixels. Also, resize the guassian blurred image with kernel size 7X7 and sigma 4.0 to 50X50 pixels. Display the original image with the resized images in a 1X3 grid.\n","\n","e) Generate a 3 level Gaussian pyramid (L0 - original image, L1, L2, L3) by choosing an appropriate kernel size and display the images in a 1X4 grid. Check the slide (03-Subsampling-and-Image-Pyramid.pdf) for reference.\n","\n","f) Generate a 3 level Laplacian pyramid (L0 - original image, L1, L2, L3) by choosing an appropriate kernel size and display the images in a 1X3 grid. Check the slide (03-Subsampling-and-Image-Pyramid.pdf) for reference.\n","\n","Note: Apply zero-padding to make the filtered image size same as original image. "],"cell_type":"markdown"},{"id":"GbqqBDBd","metadata":{},"source":["### Task 5 (15 points) \n","\n","a) Import the \"uta.png\" image and apply the \"shadow effect\" on the image. (apply an appropriate amount of blurring and you may shift the gaussian blurred image to create the shadow effect)\n","\n","<img src = \"Images/uta.png\" style=\"width:360px;height:140px\">\n","\n","b) Import the \"grand_teton.jpg\" image and apply the \"tilt shift effect\" on the image where the barn and the mountains should be in focus and the background and foreground should be blurred (any reasonable amount of blurring of the foreground and backgroud will be considered as correct)\n","\n","<img src = \"Images/grand_teton.jpg\" style=\"width:400px;height:200px\">"],"cell_type":"markdown"},{"id":"3ku8d6Eg","metadata":{},"source":["## Submission Guidelines:\n","\n","1. Submit through Canvas your source code in a single .ipynb file. The name of the .ipynb file should be YourStudentID.ipynb. (For example: 1001234567.ipynb)\n","\n","2. Import all the images from the ./Images directory. Your TA will use the same directory name to grade your submission.\n","\n","3. You don't need to attach the image file with your submission."],"cell_type":"markdown"}],"nbformat":4,"nbformat_minor":5,"metadata":{"language_info":{"nbconvert_exporter":"python","mimetype":"text/x-python","version":"3.9.13","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","file_extension":".py","name":"python"},"kernelspec":{"display_name":"Python 3 (ipykernel)","name":"python3","language":"python"}}}
